\section{Introduction}

\noindent There are different way to parametrize a macroeconomic
models.\ These parametrization methods can be listed as follows:

\begin{itemize}
\item Calibration;

\item Matching Moments;

\item Estimation:

\begin{itemize}
\item Maximum Likelihood;

\item Bayesian Estimation;
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

The calibration strategy usually relies on a \textsf{normative approach}. The
model that is calibrated is not meant to provide a full description of some
economic data, instead, it is aimed at describing some simple economic
mechanisms. This is usually adressed through the lense of simple toy models.
The problem with the calibration strategy is that is it difficult to chose the
right values for the parameters.\ The difficulty of the calibration strategy
can be summarized in a critique by \cite{pindyck2017use} of economic models of
climate change as follows: \textquotedblleft\textit{Because the modeler has so
much freedom in choosing functional forms,}

\textit{parameter values, and other inputs, the model can be used to obtain
almost any result}

\textit{one desires (...)}\textquotedblright.

In contrast, the estimation strategy relies on a positive approach: it is
aimed at replicating either some specific features of the data (aka
'\textsf{weak information method}'), or all the information offered by the
data (aka '\textsf{full information method}').\ This dichotomy is referred by
econometricians to as weak versus full-information methods. Note the first
prototype of a DSGE\ model of \cite{kydland1982time} was relying of weak
information method in a sense that the model was calibrated to match some
moments of the data. Econometrician needed more efforts of research to put the
model to the data. \cite{taylor1990solving} and \cite{fair1990full} for
instance provide some example of estimated DSGE\ models using the maximum
likelihood. This handout provide an introduction to the estimation of linear
model using full information methods.

\section{Filters}

Filters are statistical methods which allows to extract relevant information
from data. For a macro-econometrician, filtering consists of using the model
to extract the sequence of shocks from macroeconomic time series.\ Note that
the same sample, there are an infinite number of sequences of shocks and
models that can replicate the same sample.\ So the extracted sequence of
shocks of one model provide one - among many possible - representation of the data.

\subsection{The Kalman filter}

The Kalman filter was initially developed for engeneering control problem
(e.g. Nasa spatial program).\ In this section, we follow Hamilton's notation
and will set up the Kalman filter on general time series model. First, the
Kalman filter is linear, so this filter can only be employed for the
estimation of linear models. Since most of DSGE\ models are simulated using a
linear approximation, the Kalman has become very popular for the estimation of
DSGE\ models. Another aspect

First, note that when we try to put the model to the data, there are observed
and unobserved variables. Think about a RBC\ model with physical capital,
consumption, productivity and investment, an econometrician is able to observe
consumption and investment because these are available as macroeconomic time
series, while physical capital and productivity are not directly
observable.\footnote{For physical capital, we are not sure exactly at which
rate physical capital depreciates. Similarly, the measure of productivity is
also subject to endless controversies (think about the Solow's residual).}

The set of unobserved state is given by:%
\begin{equation}
x_{t}=Fx_{t-1}+G\varepsilon_{t},\text{ with }\varepsilon_{t}\sim
\mathcal{N}(0,Q) \label{eq: unobserved variables}%
\end{equation}
where the covariance matrix of errors is given by $E(v_{t},v_{t}^{\prime})=Q$,
$v_{t}\sim N(0,Q)$ is a vector of stochastic disturbances, $F$ is a full rank
matrix.\ Note that this equation transition is a transition equation as it
describes how a set of unobservable state variations evolves over time. Note
that the system in \autoref{eq: unobserved variables} is actually the policy
rule of our model. Regarding the set of observable variables, the equation
reads as:
\begin{equation}
y_{t}=Hx_{t}+v_{t},\text{ with }v_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,R)
\label{eq: observed variables}%
\end{equation}
where $w_{t}$\ is a vector of stochastic disturbances which are interpreted as
measurement errors with $w_{t}\sim N(0,P)$, $M$\ is a matrix that describes
how observable variables are related to unobserved ones. This equation will be
described in the model as \textsf{measurement equations}.%
\[
y_{t}=HFx_{t-1}+HG\omega_{t}+v_{t}%
\]


In a nutshel, the Kalman filter allows an econometrician to retrieve the
values of $x_{t}$, $v_{t}$\ and $w_{t}$ that replicate $y_{t}$. We assume for
now that $H$, $F$, $R$, and $Q$ are known and time unvarying (without a loss
of generality).\ \ We provide data Y and matrices H F\ and G, and the filter
provides the vectors or errors and their distribution.

The 'best' prediction in the Kalman filter is defined as the optimal mean
squared error (MSE) estimator:
\begin{equation}
\min E\left(  y_{t}-E_{t-1}\{y_{t}\}\right)  \left(  y_{t}-E_{t-1}%
\{y_{t}\}\right)  ^{\prime},
\end{equation}
where $K_{t}$\ represents the closed-form expression of the estimator, it
plays a key role in determining how the predictions of the model adjust to
surprises (i.e. unexpected shocks $\varepsilon_{t}$). \ We are interested in
computing an estimate of $x_{t}$ given 'surprises' in $y_{t}$ (surprises are
the errors of predictions):
\begin{equation}
x_{t}=E_{t-1}\{x_{t}\}+K_{t}\left(  y_{t}-E_{t-1}\{y_{t}\}\right)  .
\end{equation}


From \autoref{eq: observed variables}, the prediction of $E_{t-1}y_{t}%
=$\ $HE_{t-1}x_{t}$, then the previous equation is given by:%
\[
x_{t}=E_{t-1}\{x_{t}\}+K_{t}\left(  y_{t}-HE_{t-1}\{x_{t}\}\right)
\]


Following a lot of arithmetic, the Kalman filter is summarized by the
following set of equations:%

\begin{align*}
K_{t}  &  =E_{t-1}\{\Sigma_{t}\}H^{\prime}\left(  HE_{t-1}\{\Sigma
_{t}\}H^{\prime}+R\right)  ^{-1}\\
\Sigma_{t}  &  =E_{t-1}\{\Sigma_{t}\}-K_{t}HE_{t-1}\{\Sigma_{t}\}\\
x_{t}  &  =E_{t-1}\{x_{t}\}-K_{t}\left(  y_{t}-HE_{t-1}\{x_{t}\}\right) \\
E_{t}\{\Sigma_{t+1}\}  &  =F\{\Sigma_{t}\}F^{\prime}+GQG^{\prime}\\
E_{t}\{s_{t+1}\}  &  =Fs_{t}%
\end{align*}


Note that Dynare automatically employs the Kalman filter to extract the
sequence of errors from the estimated model.

\subsection{The Inversion Filter}

As an alternative to the Kalman, we can use the inversion filter of
\cite{kollmann2013estimating} that can serve the sequence of errors of any
model (either linear or non-linear).\ Our system reads as follows:%
\[
x_{t}=Fx_{t-1}+G\varepsilon_{t},\text{with }\varepsilon_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}%
(0,Q)
\]
Or with measurement errors:%
\begin{align*}
y_{t}  &  =HFx_{t-1}+HG\omega_{t}+v_{t}\\
&  =\mathcal{H}x_{t-1}+\mathcal{G}\varepsilon_{t}\text{ with }\varepsilon
_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(  0,\left[
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\sigma_{\omega}^{2} & V^{\prime}\\
V & \sigma_{v}^{2}%
\end{array}
\right]  \right)
\end{align*}
where $\mathcal{H=}HF$ and $\mathcal{G=[GH};1_{n_{v}}\mathcal{]}^{\prime}$,
$\varepsilon_{t}=[\omega_{t},v_{t}]^{\prime}$\ \ and $V$\ stacks the
covariances of errors between $\omega_{t}$\ and $v_{t}$.

Consider a selection matrix denoted $\Lambda$ such that the set of observable
is given by $y_{t}=\Lambda x_{t}$. Note that there as many rows as endogenous
variables $n_{x}$ and as many column as observable variables $n_{y}$, this
matrix is of all zeros except for row/column observables variables that must
include a dummy.\ Mapping the model $y_{t}$\ to the data $Y_{t}$ implifies
that:%
\begin{align*}
Y_{t}  &  =y_{t}\\
&  =\Lambda x_{t}\\
&  =\Lambda Fx_{t-1}+\Lambda G\varepsilon_{t}%
\end{align*}
We thus solve the equation in $\varepsilon_{t}$\ such that the closed-form
expression for the contemporaneous realization of shocks:%
\begin{equation}
\varepsilon_{t}=\left(  \Lambda G\right)  ^{-1}\left(  Y_{t}-\Lambda
Fx_{t-1}\right)  \label{eq 4}%
\end{equation}


Note that the error of forecasts, denoted $Y_{t}-\Lambda Fx_{t-1}$, is the
economic surprise that is rescaled to the model's shock size through the
product with the term $\left(  \Lambda G\right)  ^{-1}$. Therefore using this
equation, we can extract the sequence of errors recursively. What is the
difference between the inversion and the Kalman filter? The Kalman filter is
the best linear estimator, so it picks the 'best' sequence of shocks\ that
minimizes the mean squared errors.\ But if there are as many shocks as
observable variables (that is mostly the case for estimated DSGE) then there
is only one possible sequence of shock so there is no 'best"
prediction.\ However, if there are more shocks than observable variables, then
the Kalman is useful to find the 'best' sequence of errors.\ Note in
\autoref{eq 4}, we can use the inversion filter only if matrix $\Lambda G$\ is
square and invertible.\ Put differently, the inversion filter only works if
the number of observable variables is the same as the sequence of errors to be
estimated. Given this restriction on the inversion filter, 99.9\% yet use the
Kalman filter to evaluate the sequence of error of the model.%

