\subsection{Syntax for log-likelihood estimation}

First, let us take back the dynare file of the production economy and include
an equation for output.

Second, to estimate using maximum likelihood techniques, first declare the
variables of the model which are observed. This is done using the
\textsf{varobs} \textsf{VAR1\ VAR2 VAR3 ;} command, \textsf{VARX}\ must be in
the data file a vector of an observed variable, otherwise the estimation won't
start. Here, we only have output as observed:%

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginCode}{\vspace{3ex}
%\begin{mybox}{code}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\vspace{3ex}
\begin{mybox}{code}%
%EndExpansion


\textsf{varobs y;}%

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndCode}{\end{mybox}
%\vspace{3ex}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{mybox}
\vspace{3ex}%
%EndExpansion


Next, we specify the parameters to be estimated in the
\textsf{estimated\_params;} block. When writing the estimated params section,
each parameter to be estimated needs to be given an initial value, and can be
given bounds: the syntax to do this is parameter, \textsf{initial value [,
lower bound, upper bound ];} where the parts in the square brackets are
optional. For the standard errors of the shocks, we use the same syntax,
preceding the exogenous variable with \textsf{stderr}, like for specifying the
shocks. The full estimated params section is:%

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginCode}{\vspace{3ex}
%\begin{mybox}{code}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\vspace{3ex}
\begin{mybox}{code}%
%EndExpansion


\textsf{estimated\_params;}

\textsf{\qquad stderr\qquad e\_a,\qquad0.007,\qquad0,\qquad inf;}

\textsf{\qquad\qquad\qquad rho,\qquad0.95,\qquad0,\qquad1;}

\textsf{end;}%

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndCode}{\end{mybox}
%\vspace{3ex}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{mybox}
\vspace{3ex}%
%EndExpansion


Finally, we need to call estimation and specify where Dynare can find the
observed series. Our observed vector is in the Matlab file
"\textsf{mydata.mat}". This is called with:%

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{BeginCode}{\vspace{3ex}
%\begin{mybox}{code}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\vspace{3ex}
\begin{mybox}{code}%
%EndExpansion


\textsf{estimation(datafile=mydata);}%

%TCIMACRO{\TeXButton{EndCode}{\end{mybox}
%\vspace{3ex}}}%
%BeginExpansion
\end{mybox}
\vspace{3ex}%
%EndExpansion


\subsection{Results from the estimation exercise}

Dynare is now solving the optimization problem by finding the value of $\rho
$\ and the sequence of productivity shocks $\left\{  \eta_{t}\right\}
_{t=1}^{T}$ (where $T$\ is the size of the sample) which maximize the
log-likelihood function. At the beginning of the optimization, the value of
the log-likelihood function is \textsf{840.5}, while at the end, it's value is
\textsf{-916.4686337}.%

